# Cosmo-Knight

**An optional player character, subject to the Game Master''s approval.**

## Lore

The cosmo-knights have been a presence in the Three Galaxies for as long as
there has been recorded history there - venerated by some, feared and persecuted
by others, and taken seriously by everyone. Their enemies say they are meddlers
serving an alien and possibly evil master. The popular belief is that they are
the creation and the agents of the Cosmic Forge, the mythical living artifact of
the First Race, and in this case the popular belief happens to be right.

## Becoming a knight

Nobody knows what the Forge is looking for. The chosen come from every walk of
life, princes and emperors and beggars and criminals; humans, kreeghor, wolfen
and noro have all been called, as have members of very nearly every race in the
Three Galaxies. The exceptions are the supernatural - dragons, demons and the
prometheans - and no reason is given for it. Whatever the quality is that the
chosen share, it is not one anyone has managed to name; courage, determination
and sheer will have all been proposed.

The Forge contacts a candidate in dreams so vivid they may not be dreams at all,
appearing differently to each: a vat of molten metal that forms a face and
speaks, a godly version of the candidate''s own race made of light, a swirling
mass that communicates without sound. The offer is not one to accept lightly,
and candidates are given time and are shown glimpses of what may follow - death,
persecution, the loss of everyone they love, or the fall. Many refuse and wake
with only a faint memory of it. Those who accept enter the Forge and are bathed
in energies that sear and reshape body and soul together.

What emerges keeps its old appearance, which is what makes an ordinary life and
undercover work possible, and has none of the old weaknesses. A cosmo-knight is
a supernatural being whose body is a battery of cosmic energy, strong enough and
tough enough to fight a starship, all but untouchable by energy weapons, and
able to summon a metal skin at will that both armors him and carries him between
stars. Many also forge a personal weapon, which becomes their trademark. Armor
and weapon alike differ from knight to knight - a plain seamless skin for some,
heavily worked archaic or fantastic plate for others - and any single color may
be chosen, so long as it is metallic.

## The Code of Honor

The knights keep a strict code, and unlike the cyber-knights'' it has a pragmatic
streak that leaves room for dealing with real threats. What it does not leave
room for is the abuse of power. Its seven parts are: to LIVE in service to the
Forge, the Megaverse, all sentients, the innocent, nature, living worlds,
freedom and justice; FAIR PLAY, never striking a helpless foe, avoiding torture
and abuse, avoiding lies and cheating unless a good cause demands them; NOBILITY,
obeying the laws of the places you visit unless those laws are evil,
administering justice and mercy equally, protecting the innocent whatever their
class or race, respecting authority but not corruption, refusing power over
others, avoiding glory that becomes self-aggrandizement, staying hidden until
needed; VALOR; HONOR, keeping one''s word and one''s principles, never betraying a
confidence or a comrade, deceiving only in the service of justice or to avoid
vainglory, and dying with honor; COURTESY; and LOYALTY, to one''s principles, to
one''s friends, and to the destruction of evil.

A knight who breaks it Falls. The temptation is constant and the code exists
because of it - power corrupts, and a cosmo-knight carries a great deal.

## In the Three Galaxies

The Consortium of Civilized Worlds recognizes the knights'' authority and they
recognize the CCW''s. A knight operating in CCW space takes out a Bonded Deputy
license, granted after a short test on Consortium law, which gives him a police
officer''s powers and duties; he may also declare a planetary state of emergency
on discovering a cosmic-level threat, after which the local authorities must
assist him. The accountability runs both ways. A knight who loses his powers on
a CCW world is arrested on the spot and investigated to find out what caused the
fall.

The Transgalactic Empire treats them as super-powered menaces and sets its elite
Invincible Guard on them, or failing that battleships and armored troops; a good
many knights have joined the Freedom Fighters and helped inflict some memorable
defeats on the kreeghor. In United Worlds of Warlock space there is no single
policy and reactions vary by planet, several of which regard the knights as
dangerous troublemakers. Magic is one of the few things that can really hurt a
cosmo-knight, so they go to UWW worlds only when they must - and the slavery and
ritual cannibalism practiced on some of them means they must fairly often. Among
the independent worlds, some hail their local knight as a champion and on others
he works covertly to avoid persecution.

On Phase World itself they come and go freely, expected to obey planetary law
and defer to local authority. Many are passing through toward somewhere else in
the Megaverse, or hunting a criminal who has gone to ground in Center. They have
fought beside the prometheans and phase adepts in the planet''s defense many
times.

Their sworn enemies are the Splugorth, the Gods of Darkness, most alien
intelligences, vampires and demons. Their usual allies are cyber-knights,
Atlantean Undead Slayers, the wolfen Quatoria, dragons, demigods and the Gods of
Light.

## GM Notes

- **The cosmic blast''s own bonuses are +1 on initiative and +3 to strike**, on
  top of normal hand to hand bonuses. They apply to the blast and are not in
  the class bonus block, which would apply them to everything.
- **Blasts at 5x damage are starship-cannon sized** and meant for large targets
  in space. A man-sized target dodges one at +5.
- **The +4 save covers bio-wizard microbes and parasites** as well as
  mega-damage poisons. Only the poison half is on the sheet; apply the same +4
  to the other.
- **Experience level for an NPC knight is typically 1D6+4.** The greatest are
  15th to 20th level and most never pass 10th. Player characters start at first
  or second.
- **Magic is the one real vulnerability.** Magic and psionic attacks and
  physical ones - punches, rail guns, bullets, falls, explosions - all do full
  damage. Only energy is resisted.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'cosmo-knight');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'cosmo-knight';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-cosmo-knight-class.sql');
